              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The word **hosanna** is an expression of praise, adoration, or joy, most commonly used in Jewish and Christian liturgies. \n\nHere is a breakdown of its definition, origin, and how its meaning has evolved:\n\n### 1. Etymology and Literal Meaning\nThe word originates from the Hebrew phrase *h\u014dsh\u012b\u2018\u0101h-nn\u0101* (\u05d4\u05d5\u05b9\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05e2\u05b8\u05d4\u05be\u05e0\u05b8\u05bc\u05d0), which literally translates to **\"save, please\"** or **\"save now\"**. \n* **Hosha/Hoshi'ah:** means \"to save\" or \"deliver\".\n* **Na:** is a particle expressing a polite or urgent request, translated as \"please,\" \"we pray,\" or \"now\".\n\nThus, in its original Hebrew context, \"hosanna\" was not originally a cry of praise, but rather an urgent plea for help or deliverance.\n\n### 2. Biblical Roots and Meaning in Judaism\nIn the Hebrew Bible (Old Testament), the phrase appears in **Psalm 118:25**: *\"Save us, we pray, O Lord!\"* This psalm was traditionally chanted by Jewish people during the festival of Sukkot (the Feast of Tabernacles) while waving branches, pleading for God\u2019s salvation and blessing. Over time, the Hebrew phrase became a single liturgical word, \"hosanna.\"\n\n### 3. Shift to Praise in Christianity\nIn the New Testament (and subsequently in Christian tradition), the meaning of \"hosanna\" shifted from a plea for help to a **shout of triumph and adoration**. \n\nWhen Jesus made his triumphal entry into Jerusalem, the crowds waved palm branches and shouted: \n> *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest heaven!\"* (Matthew 21:9)\n\nBy calling out \"Hosanna,\" the crowd was acknowledging Jesus as the promised Messiah who had come to save them. In this context, the word became a dual-purpose cry: both a prayer (**\"Save us, King!\"**) and a celebratory declaration (**\"Hooray! Salvation has arrived!\"**).\n\n### Summary of Modern Usage\n* **In secular/general English:** It is used as a noun or exclamation meaning a shout of praise or adoration.\n* **In Christian worship:** It is sung or spoken as an expression of praise to God, most notably during Palm Sunday services and in the *Sanctus* (\"Holy, Holy, Holy... Hosanna in the highest\") during traditional liturgy.",
